-- Post-migration snapshot of the demo dataset.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: this repo syncs Oracle -> Adabas, so it needs rows in
-- Oracle to change. In the real flow those rows arrive from the sibling
-- adabas-to-oracle-migration repo's bulk load. Committing the result here keeps
-- this repo runnable on its own, without the migration repo present.
--
-- IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SEED emp_id. That column is GENERATED ALWAYS AS
-- IDENTITY, so an explicit insert raises ORA-32795 - and forcing one would also
-- strand the identity sequence at 1, so the first INSERT the acceptance suite
-- performs would collide on pk_employee. Instead the identity assigns emp_id
-- naturally and every child row resolves its parent through personnel_id, which
-- carries a UNIQUE constraint. No DDL, no ALTER, no divergence from the schema
-- the sibling repo uses.
--
-- Runs automatically on FIRST container start only
-- (/container-entrypoint-initdb.d). Each init script gets its own sqlplus
-- session against the CDB root, hence the ALTER SESSION below.
--
-- Rows: 1107 employees, 2441 address lines, 1813 languages, 3578 income.
-- VEHICLE is intentionally NOT seeded: nothing in this repo reads it, and the
-- capture layer knows a vehicle aggregate that has no Hop pipeline and no
-- Natural applier - so with zero rows it can never fire.
--
-- Source: Software AG Adabas Community Edition demo data, in its migrated
-- relational form. See the README licence note.

ALTER SESSION SET CONTAINER = FREEPDB1;
SET DEFINE OFF;

PROMPT seeding EMPLOYEE ...
-- NOTE: single-table INSERT ... SELECT, deliberately NOT `INSERT ALL`.
-- A multitable INSERT ALL evaluates the identity column once per row of
-- its DRIVING query, and the driving query here would be `SELECT * FROM
-- dual` - one row - so every INTO clause in the batch would receive the
-- SAME emp_id and the second one would fail ORA-00001 on pk_employee.
-- INSERT ... SELECT with a multi-row query assigns one identity per row.
INSERT INTO pocapp.employee (source_isn, personnel_id, first_name, middle_name, last_name, birth_date, gender_code, marital_status, dept_code, job_title, city, postal_code, country_code)
